{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Specimen Handling and Vacuum Control}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Specimen_Handling_and_Vacuum_Control}
\pard\keepn \fs32\cf1 Specimen Handling and Vacuum Control
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The routines described below are required to get the specimen into the chamber, under the appropriate vacuum conditions and in the right position to be scanned by the beam. 
\par 
\par \b 
\par 
\par 
\par \uldb Changing the Specimen\plain\b\fs24 {\v Changing_the_Specimen}
\par \uldb Specimen Handling\plain\b\fs24 {\v Specimen_Handling}
\par 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Changing the Specimen}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Changing_the_Specimen}
\pard\keepn \fs32\cf1 Changing the Specimen
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par The beam must be switched off before opening the chamber for a specimen change. Proceed as follows:-\b 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \plain\f7\fs24 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4\b \tab \plain\fs24 Click on \b Fil I: EHT:\plain\fs24  in the status bar.which will display the list of permitted actions. - select \b Shutdown \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \b 
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 Before loading a specimen into the specimen chamber, the chamber must first be brought to atmospheric pressure by selecting \b Vent \plain\fs24  in the status bar. Safety interlocking of high voltage supplies results in the Vent command being disabled unless the beam is first switched off. \b 
\par \pard\tx355 \cf2 The chamber can only be vented if the stage is in a \'91safe\'92 area. If it is outside of this area the stage can be automatically set safe before venting \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \b\cf2 
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 To open the chamber and change the specimen:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Ensure that the beam is off (indicated in the status bar). If it is not, switch it off using the previous routine.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Vent the chamber by clicking on \b VAC:\plain\fs24 .in the status bar which will display the permitted actions - select \b Vent\plain\fs24 
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab  When the chamber is at atmospheric pressure, the door can be carefully pulled open.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab  Remove the previous specimen, if there is one, by loosening the small locating screw holding the specimen stub in position. \i Any one of a variety of specimen holders may be in use - however, they are all held in position with a small locating screw. To loosen the screw, use the smaller of the two ball-ended drivers provided.\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7\b \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Insert the new specimen and tighten the locating screw.\b 
\par \plain\f7\fs24 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4\b \tab \plain\fs24 
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Close the chamber door.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab 
\par \f7\b \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Select \b Pump\plain\fs24  from the drop down box in the toolbar. The pumps should now start and take the system to the desired vacuum conditions.\b 
\par \plain\f7\fs24 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4\b \tab \plain\fs24 Within 3 minutes, depending on the specimen and the length of time the chamber was left open, the vacuum status should reach Ready. This is indicated both in the status bar and in the vacuum tab . If vacuum is not achieved within this time, see \uldb \i\b Getting a Vacuum\plain\i\b\fs24 {\v Getting_a_Vacuum}\plain\fs24 .
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Resume specimen examination by selecting Beam On. (Click on \b Fil I: EHT:\plain\fs24  in the status bar.which will display the list of permitted actions. - select \b Beam On \plain\fs24 , this will restore the beam conditions)\b .\plain\fs24  
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab \i Some stage movement may be required to locate the specimen area requiring examination\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \i 
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 For a simpler specimen exchange where conditions and vacuum mode remain unchanged click on the \uldb \b Specimen Change\plain\b\fs24 {\v PX0A3N@sem.hlp}\plain\fs24  icon \{bmc bm1.bmp\} which will perform \b Shutdown \plain\fs24 + \b Vent\plain\fs24  or \b Pump\plain\fs24  + \b Beam On\plain\fs24  according to the current vacuum state
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Getting a Vacuum}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Getting_a_Vacuum}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Getting a Vacuum
\par \pard \plain\fs24 If the vacuum ready state is not achieved after several minutes of pumping, the following guidelines provide an ordered approach to diagnosing the problem.
\par 
\par Investigating a vacuum leak, few simple rules:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 1.\tab Before looking for a vacuum leak give the vacuum system time to pump.  If you have just changed a specimen in a very humid atmosphere the pump down may take longer.  If the specimen is wet it may take several minutes (up to 1 hour with a very large, very wet specimen has been known.)  If the stage has been left open for long periods it will take longer to pump.  If the column or chamber have been washed with solvents then it may take several hours to achieve a good vacuum.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 2.\tab Vacuum leaks rarely happen by themselves; they are often caused.  If you have just done anything to the column or chamber then that is the most likely cause of the leak.  If the specimen has just been changed, check the stage door 'O' ring.  Similarly if the filament has been changed check the gun 'O' ring.
\par 3.\tab The most frequent cause of leaks is dust or fibres on 'O' rings.  'O' rings may be cleaned with a fluff free tissue. \cf3  'O' ring grease should never be used\plain\fs24 . Grease traps fibres and may cause leaks.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 4.\tab Do not use any metal tools to remove 'O' rings from their grooves.  A small scratch in the bottom of a groove causes a big leak.
\par 5.\tab If the pump down is slow, check the air admittance drier and \uldb \b renew the desiccant\plain\b\fs24 {\v 2LVYB4@maint.hlp}\plain\fs24  before looking for a leak.
\par \pard\tx355 If none of the above point to the cause of the leak, refer to  \uldb \i\b Looking for Vacuum Leaks\plain\i\b\fs24 {\v 8BZ417@maint.hlp}.\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Specimen Handling}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Specimen_Handling}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Specimen Handling
\par \pard \plain\b\fs24    \plain\fs24 
\par \trowd\trleft774 \cellx8135 \pard\intbl \f6\i\b\cf3 Where possible samples that have any oil or grease on them must first be cleaned with solvent, preferably using an ultra-sonic bath.  Samples should be handled using lint free gloves to avoid contaminating it with finger grease\plain\b\fs24 \cell\intbl\row
\pard \plain\fs24 
\par Specimens should normally be fixed to a stub prior to loading onto the stage within the chamber.  The sample can be fixed down into the stub by the use of conductive glue, either carbon or silver.  It is also possible to use conductive double sided sticky tape.  If this type of tape is used there is the possibility that the sample may move very slightly during examination, this may become apparent at high magnifications or if the sample is heavier than normal.
\par 
\par 
\par The ideal sample is one that is electrically conductive as no other preparation is required.  However if the sample is non-conductive such as plastic or a biological sample the operator must decide whether or not to coat the sample with a conductive layer.  The subject of specimen preparation and the selection of operating conditions is covered in \uldb \i\b Advanced Operation\plain\i\b\fs24 {\v Advanced_Operation}\plain\fs24 .
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Changing the filament}
\pard\keepn {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Changing_the_filament}
\b\fs32\cf1 Changing the filament
\par \pard \plain\fs24 Whenever the filament blows, or is otherwise open-circuit, a message is displayed on the screen to inform the operator that the filament has blown. 
\par When a new filament is installed the \b New Filament\plain\fs24  checkbox  must be checked.  This is shown in the \uldb \b SEM Control Gun tab\plain\b\fs24 {\v 5_0HOO@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par \b To change the filament\plain\fs24 :-: 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Admit air to the column and chamber and open the gun. Using clean gloves remove the \ul \b\ul emitter assembly\plain\b\fs24 {\v 3GEM_PY@maint.hlp}\plain\fs24  by loosening the three locating screws holding the emitter assembly in place, close the gun and pump down the system.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Take the emitter assembly to a clean area for cleaning and reassembly.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Place the assembly on a clean sheet of paper with the grid aperture downward.  Using the flat metal key unscrew the height adjusting ring.  As this is done the filament will move away from the grid.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Grip the filament pins with the tweezers provided and lift the filament assembly out of the grid cap.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Release the filament clamp screws (visible in the four large holes) and, using the tweezers, lift out the filament.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab If the cathode assembly or grid aperture is not excessively contaminated a new filament can be fitted.  If any contamination is present, dismantle the unit and clean the components, paying particular attention to the inside of the grid aperture. This must be free of all contamination and particles. See also \uldb \b Routine Column Cleaning\plain\b\fs24 {\v 7_ZQKU@maint.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Using the tweezers, put the new filament into the filament holder, first ensuring that the pins are clean.  Align the filament legs with the marks and ensure the ceramic is centralised when tightening the filament clamp screws.  Do not overtighten as this can cause the ceramic to crack when the filament becomes hot.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Replace the filament assembly in the grid cap. Replace the height adjusting ring and screw it down until it touches the sleeve.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Adjust the filament height and centralising as described below.
\par \pard\tx355 \b To adjust the tip position:-\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7\b \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\plain\fs24 \tab Using a low power magnifier and a good source of illumination, ideally a stereo zoom microscope, look through the grid aperture hole and locate the filament tip. It may be necessary to adjust the height adjusting ring to move the filament forward so that the tip can be seen.  Do not move the tip forward so that it touches the grid.\b 
\par \plain\f7\fs24 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4\b \tab \plain\fs24 As soon as the tip can be seen through the grid aperture hole, adjust the filament centralising screws to centre the tip in the grid hole. Set the height adjusting ring so that the tip of the filament is level with the front face of the grid aperture.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab When in this position the tip is inside the hole in the grid, so care must be taken to ensure that the tip is correctly centred before adjusting its height.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Now turn the height adjusting ring back one and a quarter turns anticlockwise, as viewed from the adjusting ring end of the assembly.  The filament height is now correct at 0.6mm.
\par 
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \i The gun will require \uldb re-aligning\plain\i\fs24 {\v Beam_alignment} after running up again because the tip will not be in exactly the same position of the previous one.\plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \i 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Installing the low KV Anode}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Installing_the_low_KV_Anode}
\pard\keepn \plain\b\fs32\cf1 Installing the low KV Anode
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The acceleration voltage range is 300V to 30kV. Although the instrument may be operated over the entire acceleration voltage range with the normal high kV anode installed, better gun geometry and brightness and hence better instrument performance is usually obtained with the anode lifted towards the grid whenever an acceleration voltage of 3kV or less is selected. This is achieved using the low kV anode, as follows:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 1.\tab Admit air to the gun.  Open the gun.
\par 2.\tab Lift out the anode.  See \ul \b\ul anode support\plain\b\fs24 {\v DU4P7_@maint.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par 3.\tab Fit the low kV anode 
\par 4.\tab Check \b Low KV Spacer\plain\fs24  in the \uldb \b SEM Control Gun\plain\b\fs24 {\v 5_0HOO@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab
\par 5.\tab  Close the gun, pump down, select the desired low voltage and obtain an image.
\par 6.\tab 
\par \pard\tx355 \fs36\cf3 The SEM should not be used at an acceleration voltage higher than 3kV with the low kV anode fitted.  It is important to ensure that the state of the \b Low KV Spacer\plain\fs36\cf3  checkbox reflects the state of the machine.\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Stage Control}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Stage_Control}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Stage Control
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The correct type of stage and extent of motorisation should have been set up in the \uldb Administrator\plain\fs24 {\v Contents@admin32.hlp}.
\par 
\par \b Initialisation\plain\fs24 
\par Stage initialisation is the process by which the system determines the actual position of the stage axes relative to the limit switches, until the stage is initialised, it is not possible to move it from either the dual joystick controller or the software. The position of the stage is retained, once initialised.
\par 
\par To initialise the stage select \b Stage Initialise\plain\fs24  from the Stage drop down menu.
\par \pard 
\par The motorised stage is controlled using the \uldb \b Joystick\plain\b\fs24 {\v Contents@joystick.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par For a more detailed review of stage functionality see \uldb \b Stage Functions\plain\b\fs24 {\v Contents@stage.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Setting Filament current and aligning the beam}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Setting_Filament_current_and_aligning_the_beam}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Setting Filament current and aligning the beam
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par \uldb \b Filament setting\plain\b\fs24 {\v Filament_Current_setting}\plain\fs24 
\par \uldb \b Tips to increase filament life\plain\b\fs24 {\v Tips_to_increase_filament_Life}\plain\fs24 
\par \uldb \b Beam alignment\plain\b\fs24 {\v Beam_alignment}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Filament Current setting}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Filament_Current_setting}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Filament Current setting
\par \pard \plain\fs24 Optimum performance of the SEM is achieved only after saturation and alignment of the electron gun. The gun runup function sets the filament current at the value it was last set, so it is important to keep a check on this as the filament ages, and after large changes in accelerating voltage, so that its working life is maximised.
\par The above conditions can be achieved by slowly increasing and decreasing the filament current (having selected \b manual\plain\fs24  brightness control) while observing the points at which maximum brightness occurs.  The two peaks of maximum brightness correspond to the two saturation peaks.
\par \pard 
\par The arrows at each end of the \b Target Filament current\plain\fs24  scroll-bar on the \uldb \b SEM Control Gun\plain\b\fs24 {\v 5_0HOO@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab, can be used to adjust the current. The saturation point (or second peak, see below) usually occurs at about  2.8A for a new filament. If "first peak" operation is required, reduce the filament current to about  2.35A . Operation at this point gives slightly reduced resolution but much \uldb longer lifetime\plain\fs24 {\v Tips_to_increase_filament_Life}.
\par 
\par 
\par \{bmc bm2.wmf\}
\par 
\par 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Tips to increase filament Life}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Tips_to_increase_filament_Life}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Tips to increase filament Life
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 1.\tab Try setting the beam current to 300uA (\uldb \b SEM Control Gun\plain\b\fs24 {\v 5_0HOO@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab), this may give as good resolution for less filament usage.
\par 2.\tab Do not move the filament forward in the gun as this does not improve resolution but in fact significantly shortens filament life.Use 1\'bc turns back from the front of the grid aperture (0.6 mm).
\par 3.\tab If time allows, run new filaments at first peak at low kV for about 2 hours to allow stabilisation.
\par 4.\tab When running at second peak for the first time a new filament may saturate near 2.90 Amps. This will reduce over the first 3 hours of operation the to about 2.8 Amps where it will stabilise. It is important that the filament saturation is monitored during this period. Over running the filament will quickly cause failure.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 5.\tab Check the gun saturation when the EHT is at the operating value, as the filament current required for saturation changes with EHT demand. i.e. at 5kV the filament saturation current may be lower than at 25kV.
\par 6.\tab Check filament saturation about once a day. 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 \f6\cf3 If the filament current is increased beyond 2nd peak saturation, no advantage is gained but the life will be \b considerably\plain\f6\fs24\cf3  shortened (the emission profile also does not change for currents higher than saturation point).\plain\f6\fs20 
\par \pard\tx355 \fs24\cf3 
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Beam alignment}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Beam_alignment}
\pard\keepn \f4\b\fs32\cf1 Beam alignment
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The beam is correctly aligned down the column when the brightness level of the signal cannot be increased any further (ensuring the system is switched to \b\ul manual\plain\fs24  brightness control on the \uldb \b SEM Control Signal\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1OTG14F@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  Tab) by an adjustment of the gun align screws.  These are moved as opposite pairs where one is slackened and the other screwed in. Both pairs should be adjusted against each other until the signal brightness is at a maximum.  It may be necessary to check the \uldb filament current\plain\fs24 {\v Filament_Current_setting} after this adjustment.
\par \pard 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Setting EHT and probe Current}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Setting_EHT_and_probe_Current}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Setting EHT and probe Current
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The accelerating voltage and probe current can be set from the \uldb \b SEM Control Gun\plain\b\fs24 {\v 5_0HOO@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  Tab and the optibeam settings can be set from the \uldb \b SEM Control Apertures\plain\b\fs24 {\v 295DOW3@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab.
\par 
\par  Whenever the accelerating voltage is changed it is important to check that the beam and the final aperture are correctly aligned. (See \uldb \b Beam alignment\plain\b\fs24 {\v Beam_alignment}\plain\fs24  and \uldb \b variable aperture alignment\plain\b\fs24 {\v Adjusting_the_variable_aperture}\plain\fs24 )
\par 
\par \uldb \b Setting Accelerating Voltage\plain\b\fs24 {\v Setting_Accelerating_Voltage}
\par \pard \uldb Probe Current\plain\b\fs24 {\v Probe_Current}
\par \uldb Optibeam\plain\b\fs24 {\v Optibeam}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Setting Accelerating Voltage}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Setting_Accelerating_Voltage}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Setting Accelerating Voltage
\par \pard \plain\fs24 Whenever the accelerating voltage is changed it is important to check that the beam and the final aperture are correctly aligned.
\par The accelerating voltage must be set for the type of specimen under examination:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab \tab For low atomic number or beam sensitive specimens, choose a voltage between 1kV to 5kV
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab \tab For high atomic number specimens, a voltage of 25kV to 30kV is normal
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab \tab For atomic numbers in between (say 20 to 50), a voltage of 10kV to 20kV is best
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab \tab For X-Ray analysis, the voltage is dictated by the elements under analysis
\par \pard\tx355 The subject of selecting appropriate operating conditions for the specimens is explored further in \uldb \b Advanced Operation\plain\b\fs24 {\v Advanced_Operation}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Probe Current}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Probe_Current}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Probe Current
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The probe current effectively controls the spot size of the beam on the specimen. \i Optibeam\plain\fs24  Auto Resolution mode  sets the probe current to give the best resolution. If auto resolution is left on then changes in magnification will result in changes of probe current, so it is advisable to enable auto brightness.
\par 
\par \f6\fs32\cf3 X-Ray analysis should not be undertaken when running in Auto resolution mode.\plain\f6\fs20 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Optibeam}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Optibeam}
\pard\keepn \f4\b\fs32\cf1 Optibeam
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The \i Optibeam\plain\fs24  program takes the requirements for probe current, working distance, etc. and determines the optimum lens settings to achieve the best performance from the column. Usually the best performance is defined as the highest resolution, but \i Optibeam\plain\fs24  can be told to optimise for the greatest depth of focus instead. This is done by selecting \b Depth Mode\plain\fs24  on the \uldb \b SEM Control Apertures\plain\b\fs24 {\v 295DOW3@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab.
\par 
\par \f6\cf3 
\par \f4 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Adjusting the variable aperture}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Adjusting_the_variable_aperture}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Adjusting the variable aperture
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par Optimum imaging can sometimes depend on the correct choice of aperture size. The internal software model, \i Optibeam\plain\fs24 , continually calculates the best setup for the selected aperture, however it will sometimes recommend using a different aperture. This is indicated on the\b  \uldb SEM Control Apertures\plain\b\fs24 {\v 295DOW3@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab by the parameter \b Best Aperture = NO\plain\fs24 .
\par The apertures installed (sizes as displayed in the Apertures tab) are all located on the Y axis of the aperture changer mechanism. To reach another aperture, simply rotate the Y control either clockwise or counter-clockwise according to the direction of the required aperture.
\par \pard 
\par As the control is rotated, the signal will disappear as the current aperture moves out of position. The signal returns as the next aperture moves into position, however there will either be much less or much more signal according to the relative change in aperture size. For this reason, it is sometimes easier if auto brightness is enabled during the change.
\par 
\par Having located the aperture in position, select the correct new current aperture on the Apertures tab This ensures that \i Optibeam\plain\fs24  will continue to achieve the best column performance.
\par \pard Finally, it is necessary to make a fine adjustment to the aperture position to ensure that it lies exactly on the electron optical axis. Select a focused field of view at about 1000x and switch on focus \b Wobble\plain\fs24  . The speed and amplitude of the wobble can be adjusted, and should be adjusted so that the change of focus of the image can be easily followed. This requires a fast scan speed, and preferably reduced raster.
\par Reduced mode can be automatically enabled with focus wobble in the \uldb \b User Preferences\plain\b\fs24 {\v 6T9R6I@leoserv.hlp}\plain\fs24  under \b Tools\plain\fs24 .
\par \pard 
\par Carefully adjust both X and Y controls until no \b lateral\plain\fs24  movement of the image can be seen during the focus wobble. Switch off focus wobble.
\par If adjusted carefully, preferably just after gun alignment, further aperture alignment adjustments can be avoided except when large changes in either probe current, working distance or accelerating voltage are made.
\par \page
