{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Adjusting Signal Level}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Adjusting_Signal_Level}
\pard\keepn \fs32\cf1 Adjusting Signal Level
\par \pard \plain\fs24 Signal level can be adjusted manually or automatically. 
\par 
\par \b Manual\plain\fs24 
\par The signal level can be adjusted using the brightness and contrast controls assigned to the mouse with the signal level icon \{bmc bm5.bmp\} or the sliders on the \uldb \b SEM Control Detectors\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1WE3854@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab. If more than one \uldb \b zone\plain\b\fs24 {\v Zones}\plain\fs24  is displayed, then the detector controlled is the detector assigned to the current zone (which is indicated by the\uldb \b  anchor\plain\b\fs24 {\v Zones}\plain\fs24  symbol). 
\par \pard 
\par \b Auto Signal Level\plain\fs24 
\par The \uldb \b SEM Control Signal tab\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1OTG14F@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  provides the controls for the auto signal level functions. Brightness and contrast can also be controlled directly from this panel using the slider bars labelled accordingly.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Zones}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Zones}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Zones
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The basic concept underlying all the display and scanning modes it that of a \b ZONE\plain\fs24 . A zone is an area of image store which can be mapped onto part of the image window. In normal imaging only one zone is used, but for split or quad modes two or more zones are required.
\par 
\par \b Inputs\plain\fs24 
\par Associated with each zone are two input signals \b A\plain\fs24  and \b B\plain\fs24  which can be selected from the list of detectors configured on the system. The full chain of processing is shown below
\par \pard \{bmc bm6.wmf\}
\par 
\par The \uldb \b signal mixing and Input LUT\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1WE3854@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  can be adjusted by the user if the appropriate software option has been purchased, otherwise the default settings will apply (no mixing, LUT output\'a0=\'a0LUT input).
\par 
\par \b The Anchor Symbol\plain\fs24 
\par When working in a display mode with multiple visible zones the anchor symbol \{bmc bm7.bmp\} will appear.
\par 
\par It signifies which zone on the display is the current zone. This is important because many operations act on the current zone, these include:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm8.bmp\}\f4 \tab Detector selection
\par \f7 \{bmc bm8.bmp\}\f4 \tab Frame averaging
\par \f7 \{bmc bm8.bmp\}\f4 \tab Freeze and unfreeze
\par \pard\tx355 In addition to these, the signal level controls adjust the settings of the detector assigned to the current zone.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \pard\tx355 It is easy to select a new zone to be the current zone. Select the anchor symbol with the mouse and drag it to the new zone. Thus whichever zone the anchor is in is by definition the current zone.
\par \pard\tx355 Double clicking on the anchor symbol will select '\b All Zones\plain\fs24 ' (denoted by red flukes on the anchor).\{bmc bm9.bmp\}  When 'All Zones' is selected any zone specific function will apply to all the zones on the display.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Detector Control}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Detector_Control}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Detector Control
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The detector controlled by the \uldb \b brightness and contrast functions\plain\b\fs24 {\v Adjusting_Signal_Level}\plain\fs24  is determined by the detector assignment to the current \uldb \b zone\plain\b\fs24 {\v Zones}\plain\fs24 . A detector is assigned to a zone using the \b Select Signal A\plain\fs24  field in the \uldb \b SEM Control Detectors\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1WE3854@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab. Selecting this field displays a list of the available detectors from which the required detector can be selected.
\par 
\par Each zone has its individual detector assignment, so in quad mode as many as four different detectors could be displayed. The detector controlled by the signal level functions is determined by the current zone, although if that detector is assigned to more than one zone then more than one part of the quad image may be affected. 
\par \pard 
\par \f6\i It is possible to set up different auto signal level controls for different zones. If there is a conflict between two zones which have the same detector assigned to them, then the first zone with that detector will take precedence
\par \plain\f6\fs20 
\par \f4\b\fs24 Secondary Electron Collector Bias\plain\i\fs24 
\par \plain\fs24 The Collector Bias can be assigned to mouse control from the option in the \uldb \b SEM Control Detectors\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1WE3854@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab. It can be varied in the range -250V to +400V. For normal operation of the secondary electron detector this value should be set to maximum.
\par \pard \i 
\par \plain\fs24 
\par \uldb \b Secondary Electron Detector\plain\b\fs24 {\v PIF7_D@sem.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par \uldb \b Back Scattered Detector\plain\b\fs24 {\v 22PD_35@dlgmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Scan Rate and Noise Reduction}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Scan_Rate_and_Noise_Reduction}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Scan Rate and Noise Reduction
\par \pard \plain\fs24 There are 16 \ul \b\ul scan rates available\plain\b\fs24 {\v SI1C1L@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24 . .
\par The scan rate is usually changed using the scan rate icons\{bmc bm10.bmp\} and  \{bmc bm11.bmp\}, but there are also options on the \b Scanning\plain\fs24  menu and on the \uldb \b SEM Control Scanning\plain\b\fs24 {\v R5KU.@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab to do the same.
\par 
\par The keypad keys \b +\plain\fs24  and \b -\plain\fs24  increase and decrease the scan rate.
\par 
\par The \uldb \b SEM Control Scanning\plain\b\fs24 {\v R5KU.@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  tab also provides the noise reduction modes \ul \b\ul Frame Average\plain\b\fs24 {\v 117K7K3@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24 , \ul \b\ul Frame Integrate\plain\b\fs24 {\v EQNXF7@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  and \ul \b\ul Line Average\plain\b\fs24 {\v PHG8LG@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  .
\par \pard 
\par  In addition the freeze options allow the current zone to be frozen or unfrozen. The \b Freeze At End\plain\fs24  option freezes the \uldb \b zone\plain\b\fs24 {\v Zones}\plain\fs24  when the image acquisition has completed (determined by the number of frames in frame average, or just 1 frame if line average is selected). The \b Freeze On Command \plain\fs24 option freezes the zone immediately the Freeze command is actioned.
\par 
\par There is one important difference between line and frame average. In line average mode the same noise reduction is applied to all the active \uldb \b zones\plain\b\fs24 {\v Zones}\plain\fs24 , But for frame average it is possible to set different frame counts for each zone.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Scan Modes}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Scan_Modes}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Scan Modes
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The following scan modes are provided, some of which are options.
\par See also \uldb \b SEM Control Scanning\plain\b\fs24 {\v R5KU.@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24  Tab,\uldb \b  toolbar icons\plain\b\fs24 {\v IOLVYF@sem.hlp}\plain\fs24  and the \uldb \b scanning drop down menu\plain\b\fs24 {\v 215WPY_@sem.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par 
\par \uldb \b Normal\plain\b\fs24 {\v 4LTP_XJ@sem.hlp} - \plain\fs24 The image occupies the whole image area (single \uldb \b zone\plain\b\fs24 {\v Zones}\plain\fs24 )
\par 
\par \uldb \b Split Screen\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1QEXFRQ@sem.hlp}-\plain\fs24  The image area is divided into half with a different zone being displayed in each part. Different detectors can be assigned to each zone and each zone can be frozen independently of the others.
\par \pard 
\par \uldb \b Dual magnification\plain\b\fs24 {\v OMU1V2@sem.hlp}\plain\fs24  - This is a variation on split screen where the left image displays at a higher magnification an area defined in the right image
\par 
\par \b Quad Mode - \plain\fs24 The image area is divided into four image zones, each occupying a quarter of the image window. The anchor symbol is used to select the current zone.
\par 
\par \uldb \b Reduced\plain\b\fs24 {\v BFKLC0@sem.hlp}\plain\fs24  \b -\plain\fs24  When adjusting focus or stigmation, it is often convenient to use the Reduced function to increase the frame rate without having to increase the pixel rate.  The size and position may be adjusted by selecting and dragging the raster box edges/corners.
\par \pard 
\par \uldb \b Line Scanning\plain\b\fs24 {\v BCEYQZ@sem.hlp} \plain\fs24  In this mode a single line of the image is scanned repeatedly and a profile of that line is displayed. The rest of the image is frozen during line scanning and a line cursor is used to select the line to be scanned. Selecting normal scanning will deselect line scanning.
\par 
\par \uldb \b Spot Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1YKSCA@sem.hlp}\plain\fs24  - On selection the image is frozen and a crosshair cursor appears to indicate the beam position. The cursor may be moved by dragging with the mouse. Selecting normal scanning deselects spot mode.
\par \pard 
\par \uldb \b X-Ray Linescan\plain\b\fs24 {\v 9FTMO5@sem.hlp}\plain\fs24  - In X-Ray Line Scan Mode a single horizontal line is scanned on the specimen. The X-Ray data is displayed in a profile window.
\par \pard\sb115\sa115\li2155 \f6\fs20 . \f4\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Input LUT}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Input_LUT}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Input LUT
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The Input\b  L\plain\fs24 ook \b U\plain\fs24 p \b T\plain\fs24 able (LUT) controls the transformation of the input signal before it reaches the store. 
