{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Specimen preparation General Guidelines}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Specimen_preparation_General_Guidelines}
\pard\keepn \fs32\cf1 Specimen preparation: General Guidelines
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 The size of the specimen must be reduced (if necessary) to fit the available specimen holders and specimen stages, but there is often an advantage in selecting an even smaller sample size for ease of observation.
\par 
\par The object must be able to withstand being in the high vacuum of the SEM; it must not change its shape, and it must not outgas.  A cold stage may help here.
\par 
\par It should be clean, ie free of dust, oils and greases.  (Their presence can lead to charging and contamination effects.)
\par \pard\tx355 
\par It may be treated to improve the secondary yield if this is low, eg coating of the sample with gold.
\par 
\par Any disturbance in the surface structure caused by preparation procedures should give rise to surface details which are too fine to be resolved at the magnification used to record the images.
\par 
\par If an artefact is suspected as a result of a preparative procedure, a control specimen should be utilised.
\par 
\par The specimen stub should be in good electrical contact with ground potential.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par There should be good electrical contact between the surface of the specimen and the specimen stub, ie attach sample to stub with conducting paint such as Silver-Dag and coat the specimen adequately if necessary.
\par 
\par The specimen stub should give rise to as few backscattered and secondary electrons as possible.  Aluminium stubs are normally used, although carbon stubs are used for some X-ray microanalysis applications.
\par 
\par Very small particles are best mounted on a low mass foil to give rise to minimal background signals, eg nylon film stretched over an aluminium ring.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par The sample must be attached to the specimen holder (stub) so that it does not move whilst being irradiated by the electron beam.
\par 
\par The sample should be attached to the specimen holder (stub) so that all the surface can be studied using the existing stage movements (eg tilt, rotate, X, Y, Z).
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Specimen Preparation Conductors}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Specimen_Preparation_Conductors}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Specimen Preparation: Conductors
\par \pard \plain\fs24 These fall into two groups:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab \b Metallic\plain\fs24 : these are generally excellent conductors and need no preparation
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab \b Semi-conducting\plain\fs24  samples with a resistance of less than 10\fs16 -10 \fs24 ohms can be examined without special preparation
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Specimen Preparation Non-Volatile, Non-Conductors}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Specimen_Preparation_Non_Volatile__Non_Conductors}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Specimen Preparation: Non-Volatile, Non-Conductors
\par \pard \plain\fs24 If it is not possible to obtain suitable resolution by using a low accelerating voltage and leaving the sample uncoated, then the following applies:-
\par 
\par For most non-conductors which contain no volatile components, eg water, that would outgas in the vacuum system it is sufficient to coat the sample with a thin layer of conducting medium such as Au, C, Au/Pd, Al etc.  This layer is typically 20-30nm in thickness.  There are several reasons for this coating:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Increased conductivity of the sample, thus minimising sample charge up, which results in deflection of the incident beam and severe degradation of the final image. \i  \plain\fs24 (see Section 3.4\i  Specimen Charging\plain\fs24 )
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Increased mechanical stability of the sample due to increased heat conduction.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Increase in primary and secondary electron emission.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Decrease in beam penetration, resulting in better spatial resolution.  The two important current techniques of applying a coating are vacuum evaporation and ion sputtering.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab 
\par \pard\tx355 Gold is generally used for the following reasons:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab High secondary emission co-efficient.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab High conduction of electrons and heat.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Does not oxidise.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Good granularity of evaporated or sputtered particles.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab Carbon coating by evaporation is generally used if X-ray microanalysis is to be undertaken on the sample unless, of course, the element under investigation happens to be carbon.  Aluminium could be used in this case.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm0.bmp\}\f4 \tab 
\par \pard\tx355 More recently Pt/Pd and Au/Pd have been used since their granularity is smaller.  Aluminium can also be used, but it has low mechanical strength and can oxidise.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Specimen Preparation Volatile Non-Conductors}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Specimen_Preparation_Volatile_Non_Conductors}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Specimen Preparation: Volatile Non-Conductors
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 Biological and botanical samples, by their nature, require relatively more complex preparation procedures.  The samples fall into two main categories: a) hard, b) soft.
\par 
\par \b a.\plain\fs24 \tab \b Hard samples (eg bone, teeth, wood).\plain\fs24   These, if necessary, can be washed to remove extraneous fluids such as blood and mucus, dried in air and coated in the normal way.
\par 
\par \b b.\tab Soft Samples
\par \tab Untreated\plain\b\fs24 {\v Untreated_Soft_Samples} 
\par \tab Replication\plain\b\fs24 {\v Replication__soft_samples} 
\par \tab Chemical Pre-treatment\plain\b\fs24 {\v Chemical_Pre_treatment}
\par \plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Untreated Soft Samples}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Untreated_Soft_Samples}
\pard \b\fs32\cf1 Untreated Soft Samples
\par \plain\fs24 Soft tissue needs more specialised treatment.  Most soft tissue contains up to 90% water which must be removed without altering the structure.  If this is not done, there will be difficulty in achieving adequate vacuum in the SEM, and complete or partial sample collapse and distortion will occur.
\par Some botanical specimens can be observed successfully for short periods provided that thought is given to the selection of instrument parameters, eg using a low accelerating voltage and beam current.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Replication: soft samples}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Replication__soft_samples}
\pard \b\fs32\cf1 Replication
\par \plain\b\fs24  \plain\fs24 Although it is usually only adequate for low magnification work and for comparatively simple surface topography, replication has the advantage that the sample can be totally preserved.  One method of replication uses an elastomeric material such as silicone rubber to obtain an impression.  A positive replica is then obtained from the impression by coating  it with low-viscosity polymethylmethacrylate solution, allowing this to dry and then stripping the resultant film away.  Coating and examination follow in the usual manner.
\par \pard 
\par With suitable modification, transmission electron microscope replication techniques can be utilised.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Chemical Pre-treatment}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Chemical_Pre_treatment}
\pard \b\fs32\cf1 Chemical Pre-treatment
\par \plain\fs24 This technique involves chemical fixation of the material to strengthen the tissue.  There is a large range of chemicals used in this process (eg glutaraldehyde and osmium tetroxide) and there are numerous publications discussing the benefits of each.  After fixation, it is necessary to displace the water in the sample by a solvent to aid drying.  The method must be such that the specimen suffers no physical change.  The commonest drying agent used is a series of ethanol/water mixtures through to 100% ethanol.  Having replaced the water present in the sample there is a choice of three methods for drying:-
\par \pard 
\par \ul \b\ul Freeze Drying\plain\b\fs24 {\v Freeze_Drying}\plain\fs24 
\par \ul \b\ul Air Drying\plain\b\fs24 {\v Air_Drying}\plain\fs24 
\par \ul \b\ul Critical Point Drying\plain\b\fs24 {\v Critical_Point_Drying}
\par 
\par General Considerations\line
\plain\fs24 The methods described above for soft tissue preparation are mainly for secondary electron imaging.  The problems facing the biologist or botanist who wishes to undertake X-ray microanalysis are different in that the requirements in this case are to maintain the element(s) of interest in their original position in the sample.  There is not one major preparative technique for biological/botanical samples.  Where possible, several combinations should be tried for a particular type of sample, giving prime consideration to the information sought.  Once a technique has been established, instrument parameters and specimen coating methods must be carefully considered.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Freeze Drying}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Freeze_Drying}
\pard \b\fs32\cf1 Freeze Drying
\par \plain\fs24 This is a complicated  procedure, and may not be too successful.  The sample is quench-frozen and maintained at low temperature (about -130C) until the sublimation process is complete.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Air Drying}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Air_Drying}
\pard \b\fs32\cf1 Air Drying
\par \plain\fs24 It is worthwhile first transferring the specimen to a solvent of low volatility (eg amyl acetate) as the last stage of dehydration. The solvent is then allowed to evaporate from the sample under carefully controlled temperature conditions.  As this is a gentle process, there is little chance of any specimen damage.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Critical Point Drying}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Critical_Point_Drying}
\pard \b\fs32\cf1 Critical Point Drying
\par \plain\fs24 The specimen is dehydrated as previously described and the solvent replaced with a liquified gas in small pressure vessel.  The vessel is then heated to above the critical temperature of the selected gas.  Under these conditions the liquid and vapour phases have the sample physical properties, so that on venting, the liquid vapourises across cell boundaries and therefore minimum sample distortion occurs.
\par 
\par The sample may however, undergo some mechanical shock during the venting stage.  The choice of gas for this process is limited by the number of available gases which have a critical temperature relatively close to ambient, a safe pressure level and low toxicity, whilst at the same time being completely miscible in all proportions with the solvent selected in the final stages of dehydration.  It is important to replace the solvent completely with the liquified gas (usually carbon dioxide) before venting.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Choice of instrument parameters}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Choice_of_instrument_parameters}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Choice of instrument parameters
\par \pard \plain\fs24 Instrument parameters have a large effect upon the final image quality.  To some extent the parameters chosen are dictated by the sample and its interaction  with the beam.  For each different specimen, some experimentation will usually be required before arriving at the optimum conditions.  Having achieved these conditions, they may be saved on disk (as a Macro file) for future reference, See \uldb \b Save State\plain\b\fs24 {\v I6NCK5@sem.hlp}
\par 
\par \plain\fs24 Consider the following parameters:-
\par \uldb \b Accelerating Voltage (EHT)\plain\b\fs24 {\v Choice_of_Accelerating_Voltage__EHT_}    
\par \pard \uldb Probe Current\plain\b\fs24 {\v Choice_of_Probe_Current} \plain\fs24 
\par \b 
\par \uldb Working Distance\plain\b\fs24 {\v Choice_of_Working_Distance}
\par \uldb Specimen Tilt\plain\b\fs24 {\v Choice_of_Specimen_Tilt}
\par 
\par \plain\fs24 In addition to the parameters above the following should all be checked:-
\par \pard\tx355 \tab \uldb \b Electron gun correctly aligned\plain\b\fs24 {\v Beam_alignment}
\par \tab \uldb Stigmators correctly adjusted\plain\b\fs24 {\v Stigmation}
\par \tab \uldb Aperture centred\plain\b\fs24 {\v Adjusting_the_variable_aperture}
\par \tab \uldb Correct film speed selected\plain\b\fs24 {\v 12O7D55@imgmgr.hlp}
\par \tab \uldb Gamma and derivative as required\plain\b\fs24 {\v 1WE3854@tabmgr.hlp}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Choice of Accelerating Voltage (EHT)}
\pard\keepn {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Choice_of_Accelerating_Voltage__EHT_}
\b\fs32\cf1 Choice of Accelerating Voltage (EHT)
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The accelerating voltage, or beam energy, is one of the most important factors in achieving a satisfactory sample examination.  It will affect:-
\par \pard\tx355 \tab Damage to the specimen by the beam
\par \tab Visible surface detail
\par \tab The production of X-rays for analysis
\par \tab Image resolution
\par In determining the accelerating voltage, the following factors must be considered:-
\par 
\par \uldb \b Beam Damage\plain\b\fs24 {\v Beam_Damage_and_EHT}
\par \uldb Surface Detail\plain\b\fs24 {\v Surface_Detail_and_EHT}
\par \uldb X-Ray Analysis\plain\b\fs24 {\v X_Ray_Analysis_and_EHT}
\par \uldb Resolution\plain\b\fs24 {\v Resolution_and_EHT}
\par \plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Beam Damage and EHT}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Beam_Damage_and_EHT}
\pard\keepn\tx355 \b\fs32\cf1 Beam Damage
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 If the specimen is beam sensitive, a higher accelerating voltage will result in a greater risk of the sample being damaged by the beam.  This can introduce artefacts into the image which may appear as cracking or darkened areas in the image.  The cause of this phenomenon is a localised heating or ionisation of the 
\par \pard\tx355 specimen in the area of examination, hence the problem worsens as the magnification is increased.  Beam damage  can also be the result of using too high a probe current, thus the accelerating voltage and probe current should both be adjusted together to determine the optimum settings.  This problem is usually exhibited by low atomic weight specimens such as plastics or biological samples.  Coating the specimen with conducting film such as gold often helps to dissipate the heat generated by the beam.  However, better results are usually obtained by operating at lower voltages.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Surface Detail and EHT}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Surface_Detail_and_EHT}
\pard\keepn \b\fs32\cf1 Surface Detail
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The accelerating voltage of the electron beam directly affects the amount of surface detail seen in the image.  This is most easily understood by considering the simplified \ul \b\ul beam-specimen interaction volume Diagram\plain\b\fs24 {\v beam_specimen_interaction_volume_Diagram}\plain\fs24 
\par 
\par The zone of primary excitation increases in depth and diameter with a high accelerating voltage.  Backscattered electrons which are generated mainly beneath the specimen surface possess enough energy to ionise one or more specimen atoms before escaping from the specimen surface.  Each of these ionisations produces more secondary electrons which are added to the true secondary electron signal and result in a loss of surface detail.  The use of too 
\par \pard high a voltage also results in parts of the sample exhibiting very bright edges.  The 'edge effect' is caused by the electron beam completely penetrating the edge of the feature, so that secondary electrons are produced from a larger effective surface area
\par See  Sample of \ul \b\ul fractured aluminium at high voltage\plain\b\fs24 {\v fractured_aluminium_at_high_voltage}\plain\fs24  and \ul \b\ul fractured aluminium at low voltage\plain\b\fs24 {\v fractured_aluminium_at_low_voltage}\plain\fs24 .
\par \page
